## HOW A FROZEN TEXT IS READ
Every text stands in a fenced block whose first line is `[[TEXT <id>]]`; the text is every line after the id line up to the closing fence, joined by line feeds, no line feed at its end. Its length and sha256 (UTF-8, unfilled) are in THE FROZEN TABLE; **check every one BEFORE filling; a difference is a STOP.** Fill-ins are `<<NAME>>`, replaced by program only; **no `<<` may remain in anything you write.** `<<WRITE-DATE>>` is `date +%Y-%m-%d` at the write; `<<HHMM>>` the clock of the history write from `date`, once, as `HH:MM`. `<<KEY-HS>>` is the key T-C1 created (or THE SKIP RULE found), from your write log only; `<<CM60-ID>>` the id of T-K1's comment (or THE COMMENT RULE's skip), from your write log only. `<<CLAUDE-OLD8>>` and `<<CLAUDE-NEW8>>` are the first 8 hex of `CLAUDE.md`'s sha256 before and after PART 3, from your own prints. **Slot OXPD2-60 DONE:** `<<C84-60>>` is TEXT C84-60-DONE, `<<CM60-END>>` TEXT CM60-END-DONE, `<<H-60>>` TEXT H-60-DONE; **slot LEAVE:** the `-LEAVE` texts of the same names. **Slot TICK112 TICK:** `<<TICKS>>` is `owed items 112 and 117`; **slot LEAVE:** `owed item 117`. The C-number (84) and the new owed number (118) are frozen in the texts (P-4, P-7). **This kit holds no text alone: a text that fails a check STOPS its step whole** (`STOPPED: NEEDS TUESDAY <text id> <check>`).

