**The ADF of a text** (by program, never by hand; B2040's rule): split the filled text on `\n\n` into blocks; every block is ONE `paragraph` node holding ONE `text` node with the block's text (every block of J-D and CM60 is one line, and none starts with `>`; MEASURED-BY-DRAFTER: J-D 6 blocks, CM60 6 blocks). No marks, no other node. **The inverse:** a `paragraph` gives its text nodes joined; nodes are joined by `\n\n`; any other node type is UNEQUAL (MEASURED-BY-DRAFTER: both round trips EQUAL, a one-character mutant UNEQUAL).
